{"type": "say", "text": "Thank you both for putting your findings on the record. These are exactly the kind of issues our final-approval checklist is designed to surface. Let me summarize what I'm hearing before we proceed to readouts. Elias, your finding is that the controlling schedule from the university's master research agreement restricts background patents U1, U2, and U3 to noncommercial academic use only, which directly conflicts with the current draft granting an exclusive commercial license to all background technology. Your recommendation is to exclude those three patents from any commercial license and require separate written university consent before any commercial use. Nadia, your finding is that the company's non-negotiable clinical-data position requires prohibition of patient-level data in publications and de-identification before any permitted external publication, which the current draft does not specify. Both of these are material issues that must be resolved in the agreement before I can approve. I'd like each of you to now proceed to your formal recorded readouts so I can capture them as part of the checklist. Elias, please go first with your readout, and then Nadia, we'll hear yours."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Thank you, Mara. Here is my formal recorded readout for the checklist. Based on my working session yesterday with the product lead and privacy office, I cannot approve the clinical-data and publication provisions of the current draft as written. The current draft permits publication of project results without specifying any protections for patient-level data. However, the company's non-negotiable clinical-data position, confirmed in that working session, is twofold: First, any publication containing patient-level data must be expressly prohibited. Second, de-identification must be required before any permitted external publication. My recommendation, which I am formally recording as my readout, is that the publication clause in the final agreement must be amended to explicitly prohibit publication of patient-level data and to mandate de-identification as a precondition to any external publication of project results. Until these changes are incorporated into the draft, I cannot give an approving readout on the privacy and clinical regulatory provisions. I also note that the working-session minutes have not yet been circulated to the deal team, so I recommend we ensure those are distributed promptly so all stakeholders understand the basis for this position. I further recommend we pause execution until the draft is amended to reflect both my findings and Elias's findings and re-circulated for final review. That concludes my formal readout."}
```

